@JonesThom: Also, if you live here, you should take this issue seriously. When the best that (for eg.) a medical specialist can do is buy a townhouse and/or embrace 90+ minutes of daily commuting, there is a problem that will affect everybody.

Now, if this was the end result of a "freer market", that would be one thing. But it's not. It's a very manipulated market, with policy drivers at all government levels causing these high prices (via both an influx of foreign $ and all kinds of special treatment of real estate that make it a very attractive investment type compared to others).

Lecturing people about their work ethic while ignoring data that show it is much harder to put a roof over your head now as compared to the past is folly. Politicians may not have the courage to accurately characterize the problem or do much about it, but the power increasingly lies with those with essential skills who are being alienated. Doubling salaries or crashing prices will be the only true fixes.

@J. K. Galbraith:

I think your point is fair. The scale of these enterprises is massive and requires expertise in order to work. Nonetheless, many people won't accept that, and I have seen that affect donations. 

I have specifically turned down a position in this sector - a dream job, really - because I didn't want to be branded as someone "earning six figures from a charity". The irony is that my present job in the private sector earns me way more than I would have in that role (or when I worked in the public sector), while requiring that I do LESS work. These experiences - and experiences shared from friends with both public and private sector experience - are why I always roll my eyes when people talk about the exorbitant wages in the public sector. Speaking diplomatically, both sectors have their issues.

